title: No one is appointed to judge how complex a plan is; whoever already holds the signs counts them.
pid: ccbb9aec-20c4-4619-be25-4618c0298504
body: |2-
  ITIL names a change authority, and big organizations a review board.
  Here a judge of its own would be a mission per plan, reading code
  the planner has just read. Counting replaces judging. Top judges the
  request on arrival, because only Top sees every request, and the
  four questions of step 1 need no code. The planner counts the plan's
  signs, because counting one-way doors and borders needs the code,
  which the planner has just read and Top never does. Not a new
  specialist: this is a minute's counting, not a territory nor a way
  of seeing. Not the planner unchecked: an author underrates what it
  has just reasoned its way through (my inference; no source read
  says so), which is why the signs are facts another can check (a
  decision is or is not about what is written to disk), and why Tay,
  who reads every plan anyway, may raise the tier.
